 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to requiring contractors\nand subcontractors to disclose the existence of property and casualty\ninsurance",
    "basePrintNo": "S4060",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4060-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §771, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4060\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to requiring\ncontractors and subcontractors to disclose the existence of property and\ncasualty insurance\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill helps protect homeowners from damage that may be the result of\nwork done by a contractor and/or subcontractor by requiring contractors\nand subcontractors to disclose in writing the existence of property\nand/or casualty insurance along with the contact information of the\ninsurance company before doing any work on the property.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 771 of the general business law by adding a new\nparagraph (i) that requires a contractor or subcontractor to disclose in\nwriting the existence of property and/or casualty insurance along with\nthe insurance companies contact information before commencing any work.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen people suffer damage to their property due to the work of a\ncontractor/sub-contractor, they deserve, at minimum, to have a clear\nidea of how they can file a claim for their property to be made whole.\nHowever, often times contractors/sub-contractors do not provide their\nproperty and/or casualty insurance information or misrepresent the level\nof insurance they have to the homeowners before doing the work. A home-\nowner should have the insurance information of their contractor before\nthe work is started to file an insurance claim if damage gets done to\ntheir property.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n180 days.\n",
    "multiSponsors": {
